1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,156
Open the Email Trigger node one more time and unpin the test data.

2
00:00:04,156 --> 00:00:08,826
Do the full ritual from Section 2.3 while you are at it: walk every node once and

3
00:00:08,826 --> 00:00:13,554
check for the solid pin icon anywhere, because one forgotten pin is the difference

4
00:00:13,554 --> 00:00:16,495
between an agent and a very convincing screensaver.

5
00:00:16,495 --> 00:00:21,107
Then set up your real IMAP credential, the one from Section 3.2, pointed at your

6
00:00:21,107 --> 00:00:22,318
real inbox or folder.

7
00:00:22,318 --> 00:00:24,452
Nothing else in the workflow changes.

8
00:00:24,452 --> 00:00:29,180
That is the entire point of building it this way: the plumbing was never the risky

9
00:00:29,180 --> 00:00:29,468
part.

10
00:00:29,468 --> 00:00:34,196
If real client mail is about to flow through this, one errand comes first, and you

11
00:00:34,196 --> 00:00:39,040
were warned it was coming: the ten minute read of your model provider's current data

12
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:40,423
terms, from Section 2.2.

13
00:00:40,423 --> 00:00:41,634
Do it now, not after.

14
00:00:41,634 --> 00:00:46,305
If the terms do not sit right for client content, the fix is the paid tier of the

15
00:00:46,305 --> 00:00:50,860
same provider or a different one, and either way it is a two minute swap of the

16
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:51,494
model node.

17
00:00:51,494 --> 00:00:55,991
What you should not do is decide the reading can wait until the agent has been

18
00:00:55,991 --> 00:00:56,914
running a month.

19
00:00:57,464 --> 00:01:00,735
That fourth point matters more than it sounds like it does.

20
00:01:00,735 --> 00:01:02,232
You have proven the wiring.

21
00:01:02,232 --> 00:01:06,723
You have not yet proven the judgement on your own real, messy inbox, with its own

22
00:01:06,723 --> 00:01:07,610
real edge cases.

23
00:01:07,610 --> 00:01:09,606
Trial exists precisely for that gap.

24
00:01:09,606 --> 00:01:12,268
Two of the other points hide traps worth naming.

25
00:01:12,268 --> 00:01:16,814
Connected, not just added, means the credential has actually spoken to the server:

26
00:01:16,814 --> 00:01:21,471
use the trigger's fetch test one more time and watch a real email come back, because

27
00:01:21,471 --> 00:01:25,020
a credential that saved without erroring has proven nothing yet.

28
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:29,455
And when you flip the workflow's Active toggle, understand what you switched on:

29
00:01:29,455 --> 00:01:34,002
from now on it reacts to mail as it arrives, while n8n is running, which on a self

30
00:01:34,002 --> 00:01:36,552
hosted install means while your machine is on.

31
00:01:36,552 --> 00:01:41,099
Expect it to work your day forward from here, not to sweep the archive, and expect

32
00:01:41,099 --> 00:01:43,261
nothing overnight if the laptop sleeps.

33
00:01:43,261 --> 00:01:47,863
That is the honest shape of free self hosting from Section 2.1, met in the wild for

34
00:01:47,863 --> 00:01:48,695
the first time.

35
00:01:49,245 --> 00:01:50,892
Why insist on this exact order?

36
00:01:50,892 --> 00:01:55,089
Because each step only proves something if the ones before it already happened.

37
00:01:55,089 --> 00:01:58,808
A manual run before unpinning tests the frozen sample, not your inbox.

38
00:01:58,808 --> 00:02:02,686
A folder check after activation is an audit of something already running.

39
00:02:02,686 --> 00:02:07,096
Done in sequence, each step hands the next one solid ground, and the whole ceremony

40
00:02:07,096 --> 00:02:08,424
takes under five minutes.

41
00:02:08,424 --> 00:02:10,443
Skipped steps do not fail loudly here.

42
00:02:10,443 --> 00:02:14,162
They fail three weeks later, in the middle of the trial, as mysteries.

43
00:02:14,712 --> 00:02:18,443
This is exactly how C01, the file in your course resources, was made.

44
00:02:18,443 --> 00:02:22,930
Download yours the same way, and you have something genuinely worth forwarding: not

45
00:02:22,930 --> 00:02:27,094
a screenshot of an idea, a workflow someone else can open and run in minutes.

46
00:02:27,094 --> 00:02:31,149
Be clear about what rides inside that file, because it is more than wiring.

47
00:02:31,149 --> 00:02:35,366
The export carries your node layout, your routing rule, and your entire system

48
00:02:35,366 --> 00:02:38,502
prompt: the judgement paragraph travels with the template.

49
00:02:38,502 --> 00:02:42,935
That is what makes forwarding it generous, a colleague receives your thinking, not

50
00:02:42,935 --> 00:02:47,423
just your plumbing, and it is also worth a glance before you share one more widely:

51
00:02:47,423 --> 00:02:50,775
anything you typed into the briefing goes where the file goes.

52
00:02:50,775 --> 00:02:53,100
Two habits to start with your first export.

53
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:57,480
Put the date in the filename, because prompts get tuned and you will want to know

54
00:02:57,480 --> 00:02:59,102
which version a colleague has.

55
00:02:59,102 --> 00:03:03,589
And keep each export somewhere deliberate, because in Section 10 these files become

56
00:03:03,589 --> 00:03:07,915
the thing Priya hands her team, and a template you cannot find is a rollout that

57
00:03:07,915 --> 00:03:08,780
does not happen.

58
00:03:09,330 --> 00:03:13,481
Before you leave, put the trial on the calendar the way the job description

59
00:03:13,481 --> 00:03:16,415
promised: one week, every flag reviewed each morning.

60
00:03:16,415 --> 00:03:21,009
Priya's version is five minutes with the first coffee: read what the agent flagged,

61
00:03:21,009 --> 00:03:25,437
say each verdict out loud, and keep two tallies on a sticky note, flags I agreed

62
00:03:25,437 --> 00:03:28,426
with, and urgent emails it missed that I found myself.

63
00:03:28,426 --> 00:03:32,688
Those two numbers are the whole trial, and Section 7 turns them into the pass

64
00:03:32,688 --> 00:03:33,187
decision.

65
00:03:33,187 --> 00:03:37,615
The habit costs a coffee's length and buys you the only thing that matters here:

66
00:03:37,615 --> 00:03:39,275
trust with evidence behind it.

67
00:03:39,275 --> 00:03:43,593
One agent down, three to go, and every one from here reuses something you have

68
00:03:43,593 --> 00:03:44,589
already done once.

69
00:03:44,589 --> 00:03:46,416
Next lecture closes this section.

70
00:03:46,416 --> 00:03:50,955
Section 4 starts the next build: turning a meeting into action items nobody has to

71
00:03:50,955 --> 00:03:51,287
chase.
